{
  "dom_id": "step-q19175",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=5.$",
  "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={e}^{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
